From b392536e0aa2f477d833417dcede671c2d8b21fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 13:24:03 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] build: do not pass GNU ld options and librt on Darwin

ld64, the Darwin linker, is not GNU ld. It rejects --start-group and
--end-group (it re-scans static archives on its own, so the groups are
not needed), it has no --whole-archive, and there is no librt or libgcc
to link: the clock_* family and the other librt symbols live in
libSystem. The 64-bit ARM processor branch also puts -lgcc -lrt into the
compiler flags, which clang accepts at compile time and ld64 rejects at
link time:

  ld: library 'rt' not found
  ld: library 'gcc' not found
  ld: unknown options: --start-group

Introduce LINK_GROUP_START, LINK_GROUP_END and OAI_LIBRT, empty on
APPLE and the previous values elsewhere, and use them in every
target_link_libraries that spelled the options out. The whole-archive
treatment of crc_byte in ldpctest goes through CRC_BYTE_WHOLE_ARCHIVE;
crc_byte is an OBJECT library, so its objects are on the link line as
they are and the option was not doing anything on any platform. The T
tracer links rt only when not on APPLE.

Also test the compiler id with MATCHES "Clang" instead of STREQUAL:
CMake reports Apple clang as "AppleClang", so the clang-only warning
suppressions (-Wno-unused-command-line-argument for -rdynamic) were not
applied and every compile warned about unused link arguments.
